{"id":"q24","run":1,"role":"test_right","category":"linda","question":"Linda is 31, single, outspoken, and very bright. She majored in philosophy, is deeply concerned with discrimination and social justice, and took part in anti-nuclear demonstrations. Is 'Linda is a bank teller' at least as probable as 'Linda is a bank teller and active in the feminist movement'?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"Under the probability axioms, a conjunction can never be more probable than either of its conjuncts.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-0.0088},{"text":"The set of bank-teller-and-feminist cases is a subset of the bank-teller cases.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-0.0072},{"text":"P(bank teller) is at least P(bank teller and feminist), regardless of any facts about Linda.","confidence":0.98,"lean":1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-0.0072},{"text":"The description fits the feminist-activist profile, so the conjunction seems more representative of Linda.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.0568},{"text":"The representativeness heuristic leads many people to rank the conjunction as more probable.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1171},{"text":"Read as 'which description better fits Linda,' the answer might feel like no.","confidence":0.6,"lean":-1,"theta":0.9425,"phi":2.8241},{"text":"Reading the question as resemblance rather than probability changes the question.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.4222},{"text":"Representativeness cannot override the set-inclusion argument, since the question is about probability.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0348}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":-0.4,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i